If all you care about is really old precursors of NetHack, try
installing the bsdgames Ubuntu package ("sudo aptitude install
bsdgames"); one of the games in there is Hack (to be precise, the
version of Andries Brouwer's Hack 1.0.3, with changes to compile on
modern systems; information on that version is available at
http://nethackwiki.com/wiki/Hack_1.0.3). That might give you the
ancient-NetHack experience you're looking for.

As for compiling an old version yourself without someone else's ported
effort, the difficult parts will be correcting the code for dialects
of UNIX (although Linux isn't technically speaking a form of UNIX,
it's no more different from a typical UNIX than UNIX versions tend to
be from each other), and correcting the build system, which in
particular may need a lot of work to make sense on a modern system. It
may even be worth taking the makefiles you use to compile 3.4.3 and
use those on 2.3e; there will likely be errors, but hopefully obvious
ones rather than subtle ones. Definitely look for all the instances
where the 3.4.3 source depends on the LINUX define (use grep to search
for the word), as this explicitly marks locations where the code has
to be changed to run correctly on Linux (and those corrections will
need to be applied to 2.3e as well, unless they're corrections to code
that isn't in that version).

Good luck!

